# Getting started

This chapter explains some of Gamma's basic features, including how to
invoke it from the command line to run programs and build outputs,
how each of the available options affects its behavior, how to
interpret its diagnostic output.

## Invoking Gamma from the Command Line

You can invoke Gamma by typing s`gamma` on the command line, followed
by options and input files.  Typically, you will need to pass at least
one or more options to specify what results to output and how to
output them, followed by at least one file containing a program in one
of the supported languages. Gamma will then proceed to read and
execute each program, evaluate the operations necessary to produce the
requested outputs and write them out as requested.

Options and input files can be mixed arbitrarily and an option only
has effect on the execution and evaluation of program files specified
after it in the command line.

