Document: manual

Consider for example the following invocation:

```
gamma -o first.stl utility.ext -o second.stl -Wmesh main.ext
```

Gamma will read and execute both f`utility.ext` and f`main.ext`.  If
any of them define an output named `first`, it will be written to the
file f`first.stl`, in STL format.  Because its specification came
after f`utility.ext`, an output named `second` will only be written to
file if it is defined in f`main.ext`.  Similarly, mesh-related
warnings due to o`-Wmesh` will only be enabled in the evaluation of
f`main.ext`.

Unalias: .ext

You can further control many aspects of Gamma's behavior by using the
command-line options described in ref: Gamma Command Line Options, and
Gamma itself can remind you of the available options. Type s`gamma
--help` to display all available options along with a brief
description of their use.

## Running Programs

Any argument in Gamma's invocation that does not look like an option,
that is, it does not start with s`-`, or s`--`, is interpreted as a
file containing source code in one of the supported languages.  One of
the input files can also be f`-`, in which case the program will be
read from the standard input.

If the o`-x` option is not given, or if it is given the value `auto`,
the language of an input file is deduced based on its extension.
Document: manual/scheme
A file ending in f`.scm` is assumed to contain Scheme source code.

Gamma uses GNU Guile to execute Scheme programs.  A relatively bare
R7RS environment is used for evaluation, containing only the `(scheme
base)` and `(gamma base)` libraries by default.  The former is
provided by Guile and contains core Scheme funcitons, while the latter
is supplied by Gamma, and defines Gamma's types along with a few of
its core functions.  It also contains a set of utility functions that
are not provided by Guile itself and can be convenient when writing
programs.  These will be described in subsequent chapters.

You can of course use other libraries with your program.  Many are
provided by Guile, notably including implementations of many SRFIs and
you only need to import them.  For instance, adding s`(import (srfi
1))` to the top of your program will import SRFI 1, a library with
many standard list-related functions.

Gamma also exposes much of its functionality in the form of a set of
libraries, each containing functions related to a particular
functional area.  For instance `(gamma polygons)` and `(gamma
polyhedra)` provide operations for creating many polygonal and
polyhedral primitives, in the form of functions like `rectangle` or
`sphere`, which become available once the libraries are imported.

Finally, you can also install your own libraries, by placing a file
containing library source code, in any of the forms supported by
Guile, in one of the paths where Guile expects to find them.  If you
don't wish to install the library, but prefer for example to bundle it
up with your program, you can use the o`-L` option to add the path
containing your library to Guile's load path.

For example, a file named f`utilities.scm`, placed in the current
directory with the contents shown below, will be avaiable for import
from you program with s`(import (utilities))`, provided you pass the
option s`-L .` when invoking Gamma.

```scheme
(define-library (utilities)
  (import (scheme base))
  (export double)

  (begin
    (define (double x) (* 2 x))))
```

Consult Guile's documentation for more information on the available
libraries and the ways in which you can define your own.

Document: manual/lua
A file ending in f`.lua` is assumed to contain Lua source code.

Lua programs are executed in an environment where only Lua's basic
library and Gamma's base library are exported.  The latter is supplied
by Gamma, and defines a few of its core functions.  Both have their
functions exported to the global environment.

You can of course use other libraries with your program.  Lua's
standard libraries are avaialable and need only be imported using
`require`, as is done in any Lua program.

Gamma also exposes much of its functionality in the form of a set of
libraries, each containing functions related to a particular
functional area.  For instance the `gamma.polygons` and
`gamma.polyhedra` libraries provide operations for creating many
polygonal and polyhedral primitives, in the form of functions like
`rectangle` or `sphere`.  You can use them in the same way as you
would any Lua library.  For example:

```Lua
polygons = require "gamma.polygons"

C = polygons.circle(10)
```

Finally, you can also install your own libraries, by placing a file
containing library source code in one of the paths where `require`
expects to find them.  If you don't wish to install the library, but
prefer for example to bundle it up with your program, you can use the
o`-L` option to add the path containing your library to Lua's search
path.

Consult Lua's reference manual for more information on the available
libraries and the ways in which you can define your own.

