### Passing Arguments from the Command Line

If the arguments passed to Gamma contain s`--` as a separate argument,
option processing stops at this point and any arguments following it
are passed directly to each executed program.

Document: manual/scheme
In Scheme, these arguments can be accessed by the program using the
`(command-line)` procedure, which returns them as a list where the
first element is the program name and the supplied arguments follow.

Document: manual/lua
In Lua, following the convention of Lua's standalone interpreter,
these arguments can be accessed by the program using the predefined
variable `arg`, which is a table where the program name is available
under index 0 and the supplied arguments follow.

Document: manual
Concept: parameters

You can also pass arguments to your programs in a more convenient way
using parameter defintions.  These are made using the o`-D` option,
which can be supplied multiple times in either of the forms s`-Dname`,
or s`-Dname=value`.

For each such option, Gamma will define a variable with the given name
for you.  If no value is given explicitly, the variable value will be
defined as a boolean flag, holding the value
Document: manual/scheme
`#true`.  Since in Scheme it is customary for predicate variables to
have a name ending in `?`, Gamma will also append a `?` to your
specified name in this case.
Document: manual/lua
`true`.
Document: manual
When a value is specified, Gamma will evaluate it as source code and
assign the result to the variable.  This means that you can use an
option like s`-Dheight=10` to set the variable `height` to the
numerical value 10, but you can also use more complex expressions like
Document: manual/scheme
s`-Dheight="(+ (* 2 3) 4)"`
Document: manual/lua
s`-Dheight="(2 * 3) + 4"`
Document: manual
to achieve the same effect, taking care to quote the expression
appropriately.  A string parameter would be passed with something like
s`-Dlabel='"Hello world"'`.

You can access the variable as if you had defined it yourself inside
the program, but since its existence will depend on whether the `-D`
option has been passed, it will usually be convenient to define it
explictly, giving it a default value.

Document: manual/scheme
Gamma provides the functions `define-option` and `define-parameter`
for this purpose.  They are equivalent to `define`, but only have
effect if the variable has not already been defined with the `-D`
option.

For example, to define a flag named `draft?` with an initial value of
`#false` and a parameter named `height`, with an initial value of 5,
you would use the following code.

```scheme
(define-option draft?)
(define-parameter height 5)
```
Document: manual/lua
For example, to define a flag named `draft` with an initial value of
`#false` and a parameter named `height`, with an initial value of 5,
you could use the following code.

```lua
draft = draft or false
height = height or 5
```
Document: manual

You could then override the default values on the command line with an
invocation like the following.

```
gamma -Ddraft -Dheight=10 ...
```

### How Programs are Executed

When you invoke Gamma, it goes through a series of stages in order to
produce its output.  It begins by parsing the arguments you specify in
the invocation, in the order in which they appear.  If the argument is
an input file, it is executed at that point and its execution is only
affected by the options and input files that preceded them in the
command line.  You can execute programs written in different languages
in the same invocations and all programs for a given language are
executed in the same environment, so that global variables and other
side-effects introduced in one program are also visible in programs
following it.

