Concept: operations

A program is executed much like it would be in the language's
standalone interpreter and all interaction with Gamma is mediated by
functions that are made available through a set of libraries.  These
functions can be imported and called by the program and most of them
return special values called *operations*, that describe to Gamma how
to create geometry.

The `cuboid` function for example, takes three numerical parameters
--- the side lengths along each axis --- and returns an operation
whose evaluation produces the geometry of a cuboid centered at the
origin.  Operations can also be combined: two or more operations, such
as those returned by the `cuboid` function, may be passed to the
`union` function for instance, returning an operation that evaluates
to their boolean union.

Concept: outputs

You can control which operations will have their resulting geometry
written to disk, or otherwise exported by Gamma, by designating them
as *outputs* using the `output` function provided in Gamma's base
library.  There can be as many outputs as you need and each has a
distinct name.  When a program executes, one or more outputs may have
been enabled using the `-o`, or `--output` options and only the
results of these operations will be evaluated and written out.  This
allows you to write programs which produce several outputs, selecting
which of them will be built when the program is executed.

The evaluation process begins once all programs are executed and all
invocation arguments have been processed. Gamma will first cull the
generated operations, retaining only operations corresponding to
enabled outputs along with all operations that are needed to calculate
them.  All other operations are discarded.

The culled operations may then be modified^[Such modifications are
called def:rewrites, since they constitute rewrites of the underlying
operation dependency graph.] in an attempt to improve the efficiency
of the calculation, without altering its result.  Finally, they're
evaluated to generate geometry.  This evaluation takes place in an
unspecified order^[Of course an operation that is passed as an operand
to another operation is evaluated before it.] and operations
may also be evaluated in parallel if multithreaded evaluation is
enabled.

If there are no errors, Gamma exits once all operations have been
evaluated, at which point all requested outputs will have been written
out.

Unalias: {the language}

### How Errors are Reported

If Gamma finds an error when executing a program, it prints it out,
accompanied by all available information on its location in the source
and a stack trace of the calls that led up to it. Although Gamma tries
to restrict the trace to frames that concern your program, it may
still contain lines corresponding to library functions, or functions
within Gamma's core, so that some interpretation is often required to
determine the root of the problem.
Document: manual/scheme
In Scheme this inconvenience may be further exacerbated by macros and
tail call elimination.
Document: manual

Consider the simple program shown below.

Document: manual/scheme
```scheme
(import (gamma polyhedra) (gamma operations))

(define (hollow radius width)
  (difference (sphere radius) (sphere (- radius width))))

(output 1 (hollow 5))
```
Document: manual/lua
```lua
polyhedra = require "gamma.polyhedra"
operations = require "gamma.operations"

function hollow(radius, width)
   return operations.difference(
      polyhedra.sphere(radius),
      polyhedra.sphere(radius - width))
end

output(1, hollow(5))
```
Document: manual

If called upon to execute it, Gamma will exit after printing the
following diagnostic inforamtion^[Long lines have been broken and
indented to fit the page width.  They would normally appear as a
single line in the output.].

Document: manual/scheme
```
example.scm:3:0: in procedure 'hollow':
example.scm:3:0: error: Wrong number of arguments to
    #<procedure hollow (radius width)>

#0 in procedure 'hollow', at example.scm:3:0: (hollow _ _)
#1 at example.scm:6:10: (_)
#2 at ice-9/boot-9.scm:4408:12: (_)
#3 in procedure 'save-module-excursion', at
    ice-9/boot-9.scm:2858:4: (save-module-excursion _)
#4 at ice-9/eval.scm:619:8: (_ #(#(#<directory (gamma %environment)
    7fb9ea3a6140>)))
```

The error is identified as arising in the use of the function
`hollow`, defined at the very beginning of line 3, which is called
with only one argument when it takes two.  The site of the offending
call is at line 6, column 10 of the source file, as reported in the
second frame of the stack trace.  All frames below that, are part of
Guile's startup process and can be ignored.

Document: manual/lua
```
example.lua:7: in global function: 'hollow'
example.lua:7: error: attempt to perform arithmetic on a nil value
    (local 'width')

#0 in a C function
#1 in function 'hollow', at example.lua:7
#2 in the main chunk, at example.lua:10
```

The error is identified as arising inside the function `hollow`,
specifically at line 7 of the input file.  It is reported as illegal
arithmetic involving the argument `width`, which is `nil`, but
the real cause lies in the call to `hollow` made at line 10, shown in
the third frame in the stack trace.

This call is made with only one argument, when `hollow` takes two.
While not an error in itself, Lua's semantics dictate that the second
argument is simply left with the value `nil`, so that the error is
only caught and reported when the argument is used to calculate the
radius of the inner sphere.

Document: manual

Errors may be reported even with correct programs when Gamma tries to
evaluate the operations contained in them.  They're usually still
rooted in programmer error, but diagnostic information may be less
precise, making their nature harder to understand.  Furthermore
whether they occur at all and how they're reported may depend on how
the calculation is made, perhaps only manifesting when a parameter
takes certain values, and sometimes they may go undetected altogether.
In the latter case, they will still result in errors in the calculated
geometry.

Still, enough information is usually provided to give you an idea of
what the issue is and how it can be fixed.  As an example, consider
the program shown below.

Document: manual/scheme
```scheme
(import (gamma transformation) (gamma polygons)
        (gamma operations))

(output 1 (extrusion
           (circle 5)
           (translation 0 0 0)
           (translation 0 0 2)
           (translation 0 0 1)))
```
Document: manual/lua
```lua
transformation = require "gamma.transformation"
polygons = require "gamma.polygons"
operations = require "gamma.operations"

output(1, operations.extrusion(
          polygons.circle(5),
          transformation.translation(0, 0, 0),
          transformation.translation(0, 0, 2),
          transformation.translation(0, 0, 1)))
```
Document: manual

It will be executed without problems, yielding an operation that
requests the extrusion of a circle.  The problem lies in that the
circle is first extruded to height 2 and then back down to height 1,
so that the extruded geometry turns in into itself.  The result
contains self-intersections and is not a valid solid.

When Gamma tries to evaluate the operation, it will fail with the
following error.

Document: manual/scheme
```
example.scm:4:10: in operation 'extrusion(segments(...),
    translation(...),translati...'
example.scm:4:10: error: evaluation of 'extrusion(segments(...),
    translation(...),translati...' failed due to a CGAL assertion 
    violation 'p1p2p3_2d!=COLLINEAR || p2p1p4_2d!=COLLINEAR'
```
Document: manual/lua
```
example.lua:5: in operation 'extrusion(segments(...),
    translation(...),translati...'
example.lua:5: error: evaluation of 'extrusion(segments(...),
    translation(...),translati...' failed due to a CGAL assertion 
    violation 'p1p2p3_2d!=COLLINEAR || p2p1p4_2d!=COLLINEAR'
```
Document: manual

The error does not contain a stack trace in this case, since the
program has finished executing and no stack exists.  For the same
reason, it can only describe the location of the error in terms of the
operation that caused it.  Here it is the `extrusion` operation, shown
with its operands abbreviated, as operation descriptions can get
rather long.  The source file location where the offending operation
was created is also reported, so if the program contained more than
one `extrusion` operations, you would still know which of them caused
the problem.

The problem itself, is regrettably reported as a cryptic message
originating in a check made by CGAL, the library that forms Gamma's
computational geometry core.  This is not likely to make much sense to
anyone who isn't familiar with the internals of CGAL, but it may at
least serve as a lead for further investigation.

