## Using GNU Make

You can use GNU Make to take care of invoking Gamma for you.  Although
its main function --- deciding which pieces of a large program need to
be rebuilt --- is not needed^[This is because Gamma caches the results
of calculations, so that rebuilding a previously built output of a
program, typically consists in loading the cached result and writing
it out, which doesn't take much time. Moreover, because Gamma doens't
have anything analogous to separate compilation and linking stages,
this function of Make is not appropriate.  If a program is split into
multiple files, all files containing source relevant to the
computation of an output must be processed each time the output is
built, regardless of whether they have been changed since the last
build, or not.], it can still help make development and use of a
program more convenient.

Here's a simple makefile, that could be used with the sprocket program
shown in ref:A Few Examples.

Document: manual/scheme
Alias: .ext .scm
Document: manual/lua
Alias: .ext .lua
Document: manual

```make
OPT ?= --dump-list=-

sprocket.stl sprocket.off: sprocket.ext
	gamma $(OPT) -o $@ $<

.PHONY: clean
clean:
	rm -f sprocket.stl sprocket.off *.zo
```

You could use this to build the sprocket geometry and write it to a
file in either STL or OFF format simply by s`make sprocket.stl` or
s`make sprocket.off` repsectively.  You can also clean your working
directory of generated output files and stored cache files with the
traditional s`make clean` rule.

Concept: parameters

If on occasion you need to specify different options to Gamma than
those contained in the makefile, you can simply append them to your
Make invocation, e.g. s`make sprocket.stl OPT=-Ddraft`.
Alternatively, the makefile can be extended to support parameters more
directly.  Here is one possible approach.

```make
OPT ?= --dump-list=-

ifdef draft
OPT += -Ddraft
endif

ifdef diameter
OPT += -Dd_1=$(diameter)
endif

ifdef pitch
OPT += -Dp=$(pitch)
endif

ifdef teeth
OPT += -Dz=$(teeth)
endif

ifdef width
OPT += -Db_f1=$(width)
endif

sprocket.stl sprocket.off sprocket.wrl: sprocket.ext
	gamma $(OPT) -o $@ $<

.PHONY: clean
clean:
	rm -f sprocket.stl sprocket.off *.zo
```

With this makefile, you can build an arbitrary sprocket with an
invocation like s`make sprocket.stl draft=true diameter=6.35
pitch=12.7 teeth=40 width=7.37`.  This can be more convenient,
especially if your program is going to be used by others, who are not
familiar with the specifics of how your parameters are named, or what
Gamma options can be used to set them.

Finally, here is a simple makefile that can be used with any program
contained in a single source file which defines an output of the same
name (without the f`.ext` suffix).

```make
OPT ?= --dump-list=-

%.stl %.off %.wrl: %.ext
	gamma $(OPT) -o $@ $<

.PHONY: clean
clean:
	rm -f *.stl *.off *.zo
```

This can be convenient for use during development, but you should
probably write something more specific if you're going to distribute
your program for use by others.

